course: dictionary
lesson: 11848
title: The blueness of things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Blueness is a noun. It names the quality of being blue, or how blue something is. You can see it in the sky, the sea, or someone's eyes. «The blueness of the sky made the whole day feel calm.» Here blueness tells you the colour of the sky, and the rest of the sentence tells you how it felt.
explain: Poets and writers use blueness to paint pictures in words. «We admired the deep blueness of the sea from the cliff.» The word deep shows how strong the blue was. When you read descriptive writing, look at the describing words around blueness to see what kind of blue the writer means.
explain: Blueness has a second, rare, old-fashioned meaning: a feeling of sadness. It comes from the phrase «feeling blue». Today people almost always use blueness for colour, so if a text talks about sadness with this word, it is probably an older book or a poem. Use the context to decide which meaning fits.
fact: Blueness is a noun, and it is usually uncountable, so we say «the blueness» not «a blueness».
fact: «Blueness» means the quality of being blue, or how blue something is.
fact: In poems and descriptive writing, blueness often describes the sky, the sea or eyes.
fact: Sunlight is made of many colours, and the air in the sky scatters blue light more than other colours, which is why the sky looks blue.
fact: Rare, old-fashioned meaning: «feeling blue» means feeling sad, and blueness can mean that sadness.
exercise: «We admired the deep blueness of the sea from the cliff.» What does blueness describe? | *How blue the sea looks | How far the cliff is | How fast the waves move | How cold the water is
exercise: Find the noun that means how blue something is in «Her eyes had a soft blueness, like a summer sky.» | summer | eyes | *blueness | soft
exercise: Grandad says he is feeling blue after the rain spoiled the picnic. What might blueness mean in an old-fashioned way? | A loud laugh | A cold wind | *Sadness | A bright yellow light
test: In a poem, «the blueness of the lake» paints a picture. What does the poet mean? | The lake's depth in metres | The lake's temperature | How many boats are on the lake | *The colour of the lake
test: Why might a science book mention blueness? | To describe a loud thunderstorm | To name a shape of cloud | *To explain why the sky looks blue | To say how old the sky is
exam: Which sentence uses blueness in its rare, old-fashioned sense of sadness? | «The blueness of the sky was lovely.» | «The blueness of the stone was cold.» | «The blueness of the paint covered the wall.» | *«After the match was cancelled, he felt a heavy blueness of sadness.»
